\begin{abstract}
The nature of dark matter and dark energy remains unresolved despite major
advances in observational cosmology. Here we present the Autonomous Dark Domain
Engine (ADDE-v4), a unified computational architecture capable of securely
ingesting astrophysical survey data, performing large-scale cosmological
simulations, and detecting dark-sector anomalies in real time. ADDE-v4 integrates
CKKS homomorphic encryption with AMR+PM+Octree N-body solvers, enabling
privacy-preserving evaluation of weak-lensing shear fields, BAO correlations,
and generative anomaly metrics across 16{,}384 encrypted SIMD channels. Validation
experiments show stable $16{,}384^3$ particle evolution, near-peak InfiniBand
saturation, and automated cryptographic recovery at multiplicative depth~12.
Even under extreme observational noise ($30\%$ shape noise, $25\%$ masking),
ADDE-v4 reconstructs convergence maps with $\mathrm{SNR}=8.42$ and detects
injected dark-sector signals at $5.12\sigma$. These results position ADDE-v4 as
a next-generation instrument for probing the dark universe across forthcoming
wide-field surveys.
\end{abstract}